S(7,10,r'In off-policy TD($\lambda$), which of the following is a correct way to update eligibility trace, if $\pi$ is the policy whose Q-values we want to estimate, $\mu$ is the behaviour policy, and $\rho_t=\frac{\pi(s_t,a_t)}{\mu(s_t,a_t)}$?',r'$E_t(s)=\begin{cases}\rho_t\gamma\lambda E_{t-1}(s)+1&\text{if }s_t=s\\\rho_t\gamma\lambda E_{t-1}(s)&\text{otherwise}\end{cases}$||$E_t(s)=\begin{cases}\rho_t(\gamma\lambda E_{t-1}(s)+1)&\text{if }s_t=s\\\rho_t\gamma\lambda E_{t-1}(s)&\text{otherwise}\end{cases}$||$E_t(s)=\begin{cases}\gamma\lambda E_{t-1}(s)+1&\text{if }s_t=s\\\rho_t\gamma\lambda E_{t-1}(s)&\text{otherwise}\end{cases}$||$E_t(s)=\begin{cases}\rho_t(\gamma\lambda E_{t-1}(s)+1)&\text{if }s_t=s\\\gamma\lambda E_{t-1}(s)&\text{otherwise}\end{cases}$')
S(8,1,r'You are given a training set of vectors $\Phi$, so that each row of the matrix $\Phi$ corresponds to $k$ attributes of a single training sample. Suppose that you are asked to find a linear function that minimizes the mean squared error for a given set of stationary targets $y$ using linear regression. State true/false for the following statement.'+'\n'+r'Statement: If the column vectors of $\Phi$ are linearly independent, then there exists a unique linear function that minimizes the mean-squared-error.')
