S(8,5,r'Assertion: If we make incremental updates for a linear approximation of the value function $\hat v$ under a policy $\pi$, using gradient descent to minimize the mean-square-error between $\hat v(s_t)$ and bootstrapped targets $R_t+\gamma\hat v(s_{t+1})$, then we will eventually converge to the same solution that we would have if we used the true $v_\pi$ values as targets instead.'+'\n'+r'Reason: Each update moves $\hat v$ closer to $v_\pi$, so eventually the bootstrapped targets $R_t+\gamma\hat v(s_{t+1})$ will converge to the true $v_\pi(s_t)$ values'+'\n(Assume that we sample on-policy)')
S(8,6,r'Assertion: To solve the given optimization problem for some states with linear function approximator,'+'\n'+r'$\pi_{t+1}(s)=\arg\max_a\hat Q^{\pi_t}(s,a)$'+'\nin case of discrete action space, we need to formulate a classification problem.\nReason: The given problem is equivalent to solving:\n'+r'$\pi_{t+1}(s)=\arg\max_a\Phi(s)^T\hat\Theta^{\pi_t}(a)$'+'\nFor discrete action space, as we can’t maximize it explicitly, we need to formulate a classification problem.')
